==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|équiper||to [[supply]], [[fit out]]}}, originally said of a [[ship]], {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|esquiper||to [[embark]]}}; of {{etyl|gem|en}} origin, most probably from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*skipōną||to ship, sail, embark}}; akin to {{cog|got|𐍃𐌺𐌹𐍀||ship}}. Compare with {{cog|goh|scif}}, {{cog|de|Schiff}}, {{cog|is|skip}}, {{cog|ang|scip||ship}}, {{cog|non|skipja||to fit out a ship}}. See {{l|en|ship}}.

Meanings of its derivative "equipage" may have been influenced by Latin ''equus'' = "[[horse]]".

